# ATP Phase 1 deployment environment template (SRS-ARCH-004, SRS-ARCH-005).
# Copy to .env, fill in real secrets, then:
#   docker compose --env-file .env --profile phase1 up
#
# All secret values must be supplied at deployment time. The values in
# this template are placeholders only; ATP must not be deployed with
# them in production.
#
# ENCRYPTION AT REST (SRS-SEC-001): in staging/production the readiness gate
# REJECTS any catalogued secret (ATP_IB_ACCOUNT, ATP_SMTP_API_KEY,
# ATP_PUSH_TOPIC, ATP_PUSH_TOKEN, DATABENTO_API_KEY, SHARADAR_API_KEY) supplied
# as a real plaintext value here. Seal those secrets in the encrypted credential
# vault instead and point the stack at it — leave the placeholders below in place:
#   mkdir -p ./secrets && chmod 700 ./secrets
#   python -m atp_config.vault generate-key > ./secrets/atp.key && chmod 600 ./secrets/atp.key
#   ATP_IB_ACCOUNT=U... ATP_SMTP_API_KEY=... ATP_PUSH_TOPIC=... ATP_PUSH_TOKEN=... \
#     ATP_VAULT_KEY_FILE=./secrets/atp.key python -m atp_config.vault seal ./secrets/atp.vault
# The compose stack bind-mounts ${ATP_SECRETS_DIR} read-only at
# /run/atp-secrets. For staging/production, set:
#   ATP_SECRETS_DIR=./secrets
#   ATP_VAULT_FILE=/run/atp-secrets/atp.vault
#   ATP_VAULT_KEY_FILE=/run/atp-secrets/atp.key
# Development (ATP_ENV=development) keeps plaintext-env flexibility: leave
# ATP_VAULT_FILE empty (as below) and use the placeholder secrets above.
ATP_SECRETS_DIR=./secrets
ATP_VAULT_FILE=
ATP_VAULT_KEY_FILE=

# Deployment selector. Use one of: development, staging, production.
ATP_ENV=development

# IB Gateway endpoint. Phase 1 runs IB Gateway co-located with the stack;
# ATP_IB_LIVE_PORT serves the IB live trading account, ATP_IB_PAPER_PORT
# serves the IB paper account that is reserved for adapter integration
# tests (SyRS AC-15).
ATP_IB_HOST=127.0.0.1
ATP_IB_LIVE_PORT=4001
ATP_IB_PAPER_PORT=4002

# IB brokerage account identifier (SRS-SEC-001 / NFR-S1). This is the
# sensitive brokerage credential per StRS C-3; it is redacted from all
# structured logs and may be sealed in the encrypted credential vault
# (python/atp_config/vault.py) instead of stored in plaintext here.
ATP_IB_ACCOUNT=placeholder-set-in-environment

# Market-data subscription line cap (SRS-MD-002).
ATP_MARKET_DATA_LINE_LIMIT=100

# Storage tiers (SRS-DATA-008/009). SSD is the primary runtime tier; NAS
# is the archival tier. Both are bind-mounted into the Phase 1 stack.
ATP_SSD_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/atp/ssd
ATP_NAS_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/atp/nas

# Durable backtest-result store directory (SRS-BT-009 / SyRS:SYS-79). The
# completed-backtest result store is persisted to a file under this directory;
# SSD/NAS tiering of it is the deferred SRS-DATA-008 owner.
ATP_BACKTEST_RESULTS_DIR=/var/lib/atp/ssd/backtest_results

# Durable market-data store directory (SRS-DATA-016 / SyRS:NFR-R4). The
# idempotent market-data ingestion store is persisted to a file under this
# directory; SSD/NAS tiering of it is the deferred SRS-DATA-008 owner.
ATP_DATA_STORE_DIR=/var/lib/atp/ssd/market_data

# Notification channels (SRS-NOTIF-001). Phase 1 fans every operator alert to
# email (IF-10) AND push (IF-11) — both are required, so a missing one means
# nothing is sent at all.
#
# IF-10 email: the key authenticates to the phase1-notification-egress sidecar,
# which owns the TLS session to the real SMTP provider.
ATP_SMTP_API_KEY=placeholder-set-in-environment
ATP_SMTP_SENDER=atp-alerts@example.invalid
ATP_OPERATOR_EMAIL=operator@example.invalid
#
# Where phase1-notification-egress forwards to. NOT IN THE ARCH-005 CATALOGUE,
# and that is deliberate — same call as ATP_NTFY_UPSTREAM below. Every catalogue
# entry is a REQUIRED key (python/atp_config/schema.py builds REQUIRED_KEYS from
# all of them), and no ATP process reads these: only Postfix inside the relay
# does. Cataloguing them would fail an unrelated service's readiness check on a
# variable it never uses.
#
# The operator must do ONE thing that cannot be automated: verify the sender
# address with the provider. Brevo (and Gmail) refuse MAIL FROM for an
# unverified sender, and the refusal arrives at the RELAY, so ATP sees only a
# deferred message. Verify ATP_SMTP_SENDER in the provider console first.
ATP_EGRESS_PROVIDER_HOST=smtp-relay.brevo.com
ATP_EGRESS_PROVIDER_PORT=587
ATP_EGRESS_PROVIDER_USER=placeholder-set-in-environment
ATP_EGRESS_PROVIDER_PASSWORD=placeholder-set-in-environment
#
# How ATP reaches that relay. Both default correctly for the compose topology
# (smtp.rs DEFAULT_RELAY_HOST/PORT), so set them only to point at a relay you
# run elsewhere. Uncatalogued for the same reason, and because smtp.rs already
# defaults them — a catalogue entry would make an optional override mandatory.
# ATP_SMTP_RELAY_HOST=phase1-notification-egress
# ATP_SMTP_RELAY_PORT=1025
# ATP_SMTP_RELAY_USER defaults to ATP_SMTP_SENDER; set it only if the provider
# login differs from the From: address.
# ATP_SMTP_RELAY_USER=
#
# IF-11 push: a self-hosted ntfy on the LAN, published to directly over plain
# HTTP (no relay hop — the egress allowlist admits RFC 1918 and loopback only).
# ATP_PUSH_TOPIC must use ntfy's topic alphabet (ASCII letters, digits, '-', '_'):
# it becomes the URL path, so a '/', '?', '#' or a space would retarget the
# publish. Use a LONG RANDOM value.
# ATP_PUSH_TOPIC IS A CREDENTIAL: on ntfy, anyone holding the topic can publish
# to it. It is catalogued secret and treated exactly like the token — sealed in
# the vault, redacted from logs, blanked for credential-isolated containers.
#
# ATP_PUSH_HOST MUST BE AN IP LITERAL, not a hostname and not a compose service
# name: the transport refuses a name because it cannot be shown to stay private
# without resolving it. Use the HOST's RFC 1918 LAN address — the same one the
# phone reaches over the VPN.
ATP_PUSH_HOST=127.0.0.1
ATP_PUSH_PORT=8090
ATP_PUSH_TOPIC=placeholder-set-in-environment
ATP_PUSH_TOKEN=placeholder-set-in-environment
#
# Where the OPTIONAL bundled ntfy publishes (compose profile `notify`; skip these
# if you host ntfy elsewhere). The loopback default is intentional — a stack
# brought up without thinking must not expose an alert endpoint on the LAN — but
# the phone cannot reach loopback, so set ATP_NTFY_BIND to the host's RFC 1918
# address before expecting an alert. See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md.
ATP_NTFY_BIND=127.0.0.1
ATP_NTFY_PORT=8090
#
# iOS ONLY: an iPhone gets ntfy notifications through Apple's APNs, and only
# ntfy.sh's infrastructure can send those for the ntfy app — a self-hosted server
# cannot. Left EMPTY, a locked iPhone receives nothing (foreground only), which
# defeats SN-1.12. Set it to https://ntfy.sh and this server forwards a WAKE-UP
# there; the message body still comes from here and never leaves the LAN.
# The cost: ntfy.sh learns that an alert fired and when, and the alert path gains
# an outbound-internet dependency whose failure CORRELATES with the connectivity
# loss it exists to report. Leave empty for an Android target. See
# docs/DEPLOYMENT.md before enabling.
#
# Deliberately NOT in the ARCH-005 catalogue, unlike ATP_NTFY_BIND/PORT above.
# `merge_env` treats an empty value as "not provided", so a catalogued key whose
# normal state is empty would always fail readiness as "not set". The catalogue
# cannot express an optional key; this is one, so it lives here only.
ATP_NTFY_UPSTREAM=

# Vendor data provider credentials (SRS-DATA-002, SRS-DATA-004).
DATABENTO_API_KEY=placeholder-set-in-environment
SHARADAR_API_KEY=placeholder-set-in-environment

# Strategy resource limits (SyRS SYS-11/57/58, SRS-ORCH-002/003). These are
# the orchestrator's per-strategy memory and CPU caps and the host memory
# safety margin. The compose strategy-runtime template carries the same
# defaults as static labels; the orchestrator selects the live or paper
# profile per strategy at clone time.
ATP_LIVE_STRATEGY_MEM_MB=512
ATP_LIVE_STRATEGY_CPU=0.25
ATP_PAPER_STRATEGY_MEM_MB=300
ATP_PAPER_STRATEGY_CPU=0.10
ATP_HOST_MEMORY_SAFETY_MARGIN_MB=2048
